{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2408.02565, what are two reasons why evaluations may fail to improve mitigation of AI risks, and what evidence supports each?", "answer": "First, governments may not want to restrict AI even if evaluations show danger. Second, evaluations may not buy much time before dangerous models are deployed.", "evidence": [{"page": 5, "quote": "Governments may not be willing to restrict AI systems that evaluations indicate are dangerous due to financial incentives, such as if advanced AI systems have been creating significant economic value."}, {"page": 5, "quote": "If evaluations indicate an AI system is dangerous, and decision-makers decide to restrict its deployment, it is only a matter of time before AI labs can patch the particular discovered issues and undo that decision."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2408.02565, what are two harms from AI evaluations discussed, and where are they described?", "answer": "Evaluations may lead to weaponization of dual-use capabilities and accidental lab leaks.", "evidence": [{"page": 5, "quote": "Evaluations may act as progress measures and triggers for those actors to co-opt dangerous AI systems for their own means."}, {"page": 6, "quote": "Gain-of-function-like capabilities elicitation or intentionally inducing misalignment 32 for scary demonstrations could create unnecessary dangers. As AI systems become more capable, this work could increase the the harm if dangerous models are exfiltrate into the world, either by escaping control on their own or with the aid of human insiders."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2408.02565, what is the main difference between the current evaluation paradigm and a mechanistic understanding of AI risks?", "answer": "The current evaluation paradigm focuses on detecting when AI capabilities pass arbitrary thresholds, not on mechanistic understanding.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "The current evaluation paradigm is heavily situated within risk management frameworks such as Responsible Scaling Policies (RSPs) 15 that only seek to detect when AI capabilities pass arbitrary capability thresholds. 16 This is chiefly different from a mechanistic understanding of an AI system's risks."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2408.02565, what does the author mean by 'propensity evaluations' and why are they considered underrated?", "answer": "Propensity evaluations assess the likelihood or alignment of AI systems, adding crucial risk assessment components and being harder to weaponize.", "evidence": [{"page": 7, "quote": "Propensity evaluations add a crucial component of likelihood to risk assessments, incentivize some progress in alignment, and are harder to weaponize."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2408.02565, what is a risk of focusing too much on evaluations in the AI safety field, described without using the term 'opportunity cost'?", "answer": "Overemphasis on evaluations may divert talent and resources from more effective AI safety and governance work.", "evidence": [{"page": 6, "quote": "Focusing too much on evaluations may be too costly of a distraction for the AI safety field, especially if evaluations do not buy us a lot more time (3.3). 26 All these people and resources could instead be applied to advancing ML safety and AI governance, making progress on problems that actually mitigate AI risks."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2408.02565, what is one reason the UK AI Safety Institute focused on evaluations, rephrased without using the word 'credible'?", "answer": "The UK AI Safety Institute emphasized evaluations to make itself more appealing and accepted by the rest of the UK government.", "evidence": [{"page": 7, "quote": "For example, by focusing on evaluations, the UK AI Safety Institute made itself more credible and appealing to the rest of the UK government 39 and may have protected its survival."}]}
]}
